    {
      "id": "adqvtpu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FEDERAL SYSTEM OF INDIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Seventh Schedule distributes legislative powers between the Union and States through the Union List, State List, and Concurrent List.",
        "Residuary legislative powers are vested exclusively in the Centre under the Indian Constitution.",
        "Indian federalism is uniquely characterized as 'Quasi-Federal', combining strong centralizing features with a federal structure.",
        "Cooperative federalism fosters mutual coordination between Centre and States, while competitive federalism drives performance among States.",
        "The Supreme Court of India serves as an independent arbiter to enforce the division of powers and resolve inter-governmental disputes."
      ],
      "id": "db1e8dt"
    },
